  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We aim to find a step function h on [0,2] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C5. We use local optimization on several discrete grid sizes (even number of points) and employ a soft-max gradient approximation for the non-convex problem. Our approach leverages the previous record solution (initial_h_values) as a starting point but explores different grid sizes and initial conditions, including random restarts and grid interpolation, to escape local minima. We optimize the Adam algorithm using temperature-scheduled exponential weighting to balance exploration and exploitation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Variables: step function h(x) represented as grid values of length N (even)\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i, \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (where dx=2.0/N)\n   - Objective: minimize max_k [\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx] (evaluated via discrete correlation)\n   - Optimization: Adam algorithm with:\n        \u2022 Soft-max gradient approximation: F(h) = ln(max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx + \u03b5\u03c4)\n        \u2022 Temperature schedule: \u03c4(t) = \u03c4\u2080 * (\u03c4\u2081/\u03c4\u2080)^(t/T) over optimization steps T\n        \u2022 Clipped exponential weights for lag contributions\n        \u2022 Projection steps to enforce \u2211h_i = N/2\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Grid setup: Even number of points N in candidate sizes [46,92,380,572,760,1000]\n   - For each N:\n        \u2022 Initial projection: x_grid = [0,2/N,...,2], h0 = interpolate(project(initial_h_values, N/2))\n        \u2022 Adam optimization phases:\n            1. Coarse phase (temp. \u03c40=1-30, lr0=0.03, decay=0.98)\n            2. Medium phase (\u03c40=30-150, lr0=0.01, decay=0.985)\n            3. Fine phase (\u03c40=150-600, lr0=0.003, decay=0.99)\n        \u2022 After each phase:\n            \u2022 Keep best solution via snapshot storage\n            \u2022 Project to feasible region [0,1]\u00d7(N/2)\n            \u2022 Evaluate C5 for all lags via correlation\n   - Random restarts: Apply small Gaussian perturbations with re-projection\n   - Metric: Track minimal C5 across all attempts, no need for validation since directly comparable to record\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Primary variation: Temperature schedule (\u03c4(t)) = \u03c4\u2080 * (\u03c4\u2081/\u03c4\u2080)^(t/T)\n   - Secondary variations:\n        \u2022 Initialization size: Picked optimally based on available time\n        \u2022 Learning rate decay: Decay factor 0.98-0.99 based on time allocation\n        \u2022 Random restart rate: 20% chance at 10% of deadline\n        \u2022 Projection tolerance: 1e-3 maximum projection error\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Estimate time per optimization: O(N\u00b2) for correlation + O(T) iterations\n   - Allocate time by:\n        \u2022 Primary run: N=380 (1st priority)\n        \u2022 Secondary run: N=46 (fallback if time remains)\n        \u2022 Time slicing: Break phases if 80% deadline reached\n        \u2022 Fallback: Return best solution if optimization fails\n   - Handling issues:\n        \u2022 NaNs: Skip evaluations if gradient diverges\n        \u2022 Feasibility: Project every 50 optimization steps\n        \u2022 Hard constraints: Use grid-clipping projection\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n   - Requirements: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i, sum(h[i]) * dx = 1\n   - Performance: Monitor best solution across all sizes\n   - C5 evaluation: Use np.correlate with mode=\"full\"\n   - Deadline handling: Compute min C5, return immediately upon timeout",
